# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mathematical formalism for the latest version of Qiskit noise learning"
msgstr "Matematický formalismus pro nejnovější verzi Qiskit noise learning"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mathematical formalism"
msgstr "Matematický formalismus"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title #
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mathematical formalism {#mathematical-formalism}"
msgstr "Matematický formalismus {#mathematical-formalism}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Given the task of learning Pauli noise for a set of Clifford gates, a common analysis technique is to track how individual Pauli operators are transformed through a sequence of gate applications (under the assumption that the gates are Clifford and the noise is a Pauli channel, a single Pauli will always be mapped to another Pauli up to a scalar that is a function of the noise). Obviously, the evolution of *any* state under such a sequence can be captured by a linear combination of such trajectories, but under the assumption that we always prepare the state in a Pauli eigenstate, and always measure and post-process the results to compute the expectation value of a Pauli operator, the relationship between the expectation value and the noise model parameters will *always* depend on only a single such trajectory. This follows from the simple fact that the initial state is a linear combination of Pauli operators, each gate and noise model maps Paulis to Paulis and preserves their orthogonality, and the final expectation value “selects” only one of the Paulis in the final decomposition before measurement.\n"
msgstr "Při úloze učení Pauliho šumu pro množinu Cliffordových hradel je běžnou analytickou technikou sledovat, jak se jednotlivé Pauliho operátory transformují posloupností aplikací hradel (za předpokladu, že hradla jsou Cliffordova a šum je Pauliho kanál, bude se jediný Pauliho operátor vždy zobrazovat na jiný Pauliho operátor až na skalár, který je funkcí šumu). Evoluci *libovolného* stavu touto posloupností lze zjevně zachytit lineární kombinací takových trajektorií, ale za předpokladu, že stav vždy připravujeme jako Pauliho vlastní stav a výsledky vždy měříme a zpracováváme tak, abychom spočítali střední hodnotu Pauliho operátoru, bude vztah mezi střední hodnotou a parametry šumového modelu *vždy* záviset pouze na jediné takové trajektorii. To plyne z jednoduchého faktu, že počáteční stav je lineární kombinací Pauliho operátorů, každé hradlo a šumový model zobrazují Pauliho operátory na Pauliho operátory a zachovávají jejich ortogonalitu a konečná střední hodnota „vybírá“ před měřením pouze jeden z Pauliho operátorů v konečném rozkladu.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This type of reasoning appears in many parallel research tracks in noise learning, including in the Pauli gate-set learning literature \\[[1](#id17 \"Senrui Chen, Zhihan Zhang, Liang Jiang, and Steven T. Flammia. Efficient Self-Consistent Learning of Gate Set Pauli Noise. PRX Quantum, 7(1):010305, 2026. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/1pnv-t9px, doi:10.1103/1pnv-t9px.\"), [2](#id20 \"Edward H. Chen, Senrui Chen, Laurin E. Fischer, Andrew Eddins, Luke C. G. Govia, Brad Mitchell, Andre He, Youngseok Kim, Liang Jiang, and Alireza Seif. Disambiguating pauli noise in quantum computers. PRX Quantum, pages, 2026. doi:10.1103/69wc-gzl6.\")], the ACES literature \\[[3](#id15 \"Steven T. Flammia. Averaged Circuit Eigenvalue Sampling. In 17th Conference on the Theory of Quantum Computation, Communication and Cryptography (TQC 2022), volume 232 of Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs), 4:1–4:10. Dagstuhl, Germany, 2022. Schloss Dagstuhl – Leibniz-Zentrum für Informatik. doi:10.4230/LIPIcs.TQC.2022.4.\")], and the cycle benchmarking literature \\[[4](#id14 \"Alexander Erhard, Joel J. Wallman, Lukas Postler, Michael Meth, Roman Stricker, Esteban A. Martinez, Philipp Schindler, Thomas Monz, Joseph Emerson, and Rainer Blatt. Characterizing large-scale quantum computers via cycle benchmarking. Nature Communications, 10(1):5347, 2019. doi:10.1038/s41467-019-13068-7.\")]. This reasoning was further generalized in \\[[5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")] to include Clifford-MCM gates (a Clifford gate followed by a projective mid-circuit measurement). This package most closely follows \\[[1](#id17 \"Senrui Chen, Zhihan Zhang, Liang Jiang, and Steven T. Flammia. Efficient Self-Consistent Learning of Gate Set Pauli Noise. PRX Quantum, 7(1):010305, 2026. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/1pnv-t9px, doi:10.1103/1pnv-t9px.\"), [5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")]. While not explicitly named, we adopt the *Pattern Transfer Graph* (PTG) formalism for describing how Pauli operators evolve through learning circuits, providing a direct data representation of *paths* through the graph.\n"
msgstr "Tento typ úvah se objevuje v mnoha paralelních výzkumných liniích v učení šumu, včetně literatury o učení Pauliho šumu množin hradel \\[[1](#id17 \"Senrui Chen, Zhihan Zhang, Liang Jiang, and Steven T. Flammia. Efficient Self-Consistent Learning of Gate Set Pauli Noise. PRX Quantum, 7(1):010305, 2026. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/1pnv-t9px, doi:10.1103/1pnv-t9px.\"), [2](#id20 \"Edward H. Chen, Senrui Chen, Laurin E. Fischer, Andrew Eddins, Luke C. G. Govia, Brad Mitchell, Andre He, Youngseok Kim, Liang Jiang, and Alireza Seif. Disambiguating pauli noise in quantum computers. PRX Quantum, pages, 2026. doi:10.1103/69wc-gzl6.\")], literatury o ACES \\[[3](#id15 \"Steven T. Flammia. Averaged Circuit Eigenvalue Sampling. In 17th Conference on the Theory of Quantum Computation, Communication and Cryptography (TQC 2022), volume 232 of Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs), 4:1–4:10. Dagstuhl, Germany, 2022. Schloss Dagstuhl – Leibniz-Zentrum für Informatik. doi:10.4230/LIPIcs.TQC.2022.4.\")] a literatury o cycle benchmarkingu \\[[4](#id14 \"Alexander Erhard, Joel J. Wallman, Lukas Postler, Michael Meth, Roman Stricker, Esteban A. Martinez, Philipp Schindler, Thomas Monz, Joseph Emerson, and Rainer Blatt. Characterizing large-scale quantum computers via cycle benchmarking. Nature Communications, 10(1):5347, 2019. doi:10.1038/s41467-019-13068-7.\")]. Tyto úvahy byly dále zobecněny v \\[[5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")] na hradla Clifford-MCM (Cliffordovo hradlo následované projektivním měřením uprostřed obvodu). Tento balíček nejvíce vychází z \\[[1](#id17 \"Senrui Chen, Zhihan Zhang, Liang Jiang, and Steven T. Flammia. Efficient Self-Consistent Learning of Gate Set Pauli Noise. PRX Quantum, 7(1):010305, 2026. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/1pnv-t9px, doi:10.1103/1pnv-t9px.\"), [5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")]. I když to není explicitně pojmenováno, přijímáme formalismus *Pattern Transfer Graph* (PTG) pro popis toho, jak se Pauliho operátory vyvíjejí učícími obvody, což poskytuje přímou datovou reprezentaci *cest* grafem.\n"

